From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valentin rameau <valentin.rameau@outlook.fr>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:02:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 15/33] Trip off-rails on leaving ROM, and report the stack
 leaving page 1

Page 0/1 was too narrow for a LoROM title with no RAM-resident code. Banks
$00-$3F and $80-$BF hold ROM only at $8000-$FFFF; everything below is WRAM,
registers or open bus. A capture caught a jump from $03:0258 to $03:01DA, but
$03:02xx is WRAM as well, so execution had already left ROM and the approach
held nothing but interpreted RAM bytes. Trip on the departure from ROM.

That crossing also ran with S=00FF, outside the stack page, which is its own
fault and precedes any control-flow damage. Report the first step that takes S
out of $0100-$01FF.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 runner/src/snes/interp_bridge.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/runner/src/snes/interp_bridge.c b/runner/src/snes/interp_bridge.c
index 1a389fd..13906d9 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/interp_bridge.c
+++ b/runner/src/snes/interp_bridge.c
@@ -1847,7 +1847,17 @@ static int _interp_run_core(CpuState *cpu, uint32_t entry_pc24,
                * address like $03:01F1 and looks legitimate. This title keeps
                * no code there -- direct page and stack only -- so treat it as
                * a crossing and capture the approach that produced it. */
-              if ((pc_before & 0xFFFFu) < 0x0200u) _mapped = 0;
+              /* This is a LoROM title with no RAM-resident code: banks
+               * $00-$3F and $80-$BF hold ROM only at $8000-$FFFF, and
+               * everything below that is WRAM, the register file or open bus.
+               * Page 0/1 was too narrow -- the capture caught a jump from
+               * $03:0258 to $03:01DA, but $03:02xx is WRAM too, so execution
+               * had already left ROM well before that and the approach showed
+               * nothing but interpreted RAM garbage. Trip on the departure
+               * from ROM itself. */
+              if ((_bank <= 0x3Fu || (_bank >= 0x80u && _bank <= 0xBFu)) &&
+                  (pc_before & 0xFFFFu) < 0x8000u)
+                _mapped = 0;
               if (s_prev_mapped && !_mapped)
                 interp_bridge_note_offrails_vita(s_prev_pc, s_prev_op, s_prev_sp,
                                                  pc_before, op);
@@ -1858,6 +1868,21 @@ static int _interp_run_core(CpuState *cpu, uint32_t entry_pc24,
                * bands' BVC spin -- so a single mis-step into that byte flips
                * the CPU into emulation and corrupts everything after. Record
                * the step that does it. */
+              /* The crossing above ran with sp=00FF: the stack had already
+               * grown down out of page 1, roughly 254 bytes of unbalanced
+               * pushes. That is its own fault and happens before the PC goes
+               * anywhere, so report the step that first takes S out of
+               * $0100-$01FF. */
+              { extern void interp_bridge_note_stack_escape_vita(
+                    uint32_t pc, uint8_t op, uint16_t sp);
+                static int s_stack_escaped;
+                if (!s_stack_escaped && (in.sp < 0x0100u || in.sp > 0x01FFu)) {
+                  s_stack_escaped = 1;
+                  interp_bridge_note_stack_escape_vita(s_prev_pc, s_prev_op,
+                                                       in.sp);
+                }
+              }
+
               { extern void interp_bridge_note_emulation_vita(
                     uint32_t pc, uint8_t op, uint16_t sp, uint8_t to_e);
                 static int s_prev_e = -1;
